### TITLE ###
integer channel reports values plainly
### DESCRIPTION ###
A diff of "1" against "0" is noise, so integers are reported as want/got.
### KIND ###
int
### CHANNEL ###
EXIT
### WANT ###
2
### GOT ###
0
### REPORT ###
EXIT mismatch: want 2, got 0
  at snapshots/example.snap:12
  to accept: -update=snapshots/example.snap

### TITLE ###
raw channel makes invisible bytes visible
### DESCRIPTION ###
The whole point of a raw channel is bytes a verbatim file cannot show, so its
diff quotes each line rather than printing it.
### KIND ###
raw
### WANT ###
hello
### GOT ###
\"hello "
### REPORT ###
STDOUT mismatch:
1 │ - "hello" │ 1 │ + "hello "

  at snapshots/example.snap:12
  to accept: -update=snapshots/example.snap

### TITLE ###
stale section explains what to do
### KIND ###
stale
### CHANNEL ###
STDERR
### REPORT ###
STDERR is present in the file but the runner never set it for the case, so the section asserts nothing.
Set the channel in the runner, or delete the section.
  at snapshots/example.snap:12
  to accept: -update=snapshots/example.snap

### TITLE ###
per-step mismatch names its step
### CHANNEL ###
RESULT
### STEP ###
HOVER
### WANT ###
`foo`: int
### GOT ###
`foo`: str
### REPORT ###
RESULT mismatch at step 2 (HOVER):
1 │ - `foo`: int │ 1 │ + `foo`: str

  at snapshots/example.snap:12
  to accept: -update=snapshots/example.snap
